  "text": {
    "entries": 14,
    "proseEntries": 12,
    "sectionAnchors": [
      {
        "before": "An object or system that can respond to a periodic external force can undergo forced oscillations, as a periodically driven sieve does in a screening machine.\nIts actual response also depends on its supports, constraints, inertia, and energy dissipation;\nnot every object subjected to any periodic force must oscillate in the same way or with the same amplitude.",
        "after": "A system capable of free oscillations is especially worth studying under external driving because it has its own natural frequency.\nThe source uses a baby in a cradle as an everyday example to illustrate rhythmic pushing.\nHere this is a physical analogy for oscillations, not advice about childcare or carrying out the motion."
      },
      {
        "before": "The location and size of the maximum response also depend on resistance and the way the system is driven;\nan approximate condition is not an exact conclusion for every real system.",
        "after": "We now study forced oscillations using a system of springs and a small ball that can undergo free oscillations.\nIn Figure $17$, the end of a spring is connected to a rope that passes over a pulley, and the rope's other end is connected to an off-center pin on a disk."
      }
    ],
